.TH CRYPT 3
.SH NAME
crypt, setkey, encrypt, getpass \- passwords
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B char *crypt(key, salt)
.B char *key, *salt;
.PP
.B setkey(key)
.B char *key;
.PP
.B encrypt(block, edflag)
.B char *block;
.PP
.B char *getpass(prompt)
.B char *prompt;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B crypt
is the UNIX password hash, as /etc/passwd keeps passwords: the Data
Encryption Standard, its key the first eight characters of
.IR key ,
seven bits each, and its tables changed by a two-character
.I salt
from the characters . / 0\-9 A\-Z a\-z; a block of zeros is encrypted 25
times. The result is 13 characters, the salt and then the 64 bits, six at
a time, in the same characters. A password is checked by hashing what was
typed with the stored hash as the salt:
.PP
.nf
	if (strcmp(crypt(typed, stored), stored) == 0)
.fi
.PP
The result is in a static area, written over by the next call.
.PP
.B setkey
and
.B encrypt
are DES itself, on arrays of 64 chars, each 0 or 1:
.B setkey
takes the key,
.B encrypt
encrypts
.I block
in place, or decrypts it if
.I edflag
is not 0.
.PP
.B getpass
prints
.I prompt
on /dev/tty and reads a line from it with the echo off, so that the
password does not show, and returns its first eight characters (all
that crypt uses). The interrupt key is ignored while it reads.
.SH SEE ALSO
passwd(1), sudo(1)
.SH HISTORY
These functions appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
.SH AUTHORS
These were written for the xenix86-devsys C library, from the published
standard.
.SH BUGS
On a 4.77 MHz 8088 a password takes about a second, as XENIX's own does.
